KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp20
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ const T & parameter: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ two template parameters: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
The parser skips a lambda's template-parameter-list, and the type checker
treats a lambda as generic only when a parameter's type is `auto' or a
bare name: `const T &' is neither, so T is "unknown".  Generic lambdas are
compiled as an int instance plus per-call substitution rather than as a
closure with an operator() template.  libc++'s __synth_three_way (used by
std::tuple's operator<=> and so by views::take) has this shape, and
cpp20_ranges_basic fails on macOS.
